# Every checkout is byte-identical on every host.
#
# LexLean refuses a carriage return in its own project and source files
# (`LLC0101`, SPEC.md §12.1), the golden files and every `expected/` oracle are
# compared byte for byte (§28.3, §28.4), and the compiler-semantics ID is a
# digest over the committed language data (§21.2). Git's `core.autocrlf` is on
# by default in the Windows runner image, so a Windows checkout without this
# file rewrites `lexlean.toml` and every `.lex.tex` to CRLF and the compiler
# rejects its own repository. Pinning the working-tree ending here rather than
# relying on client configuration is what makes the §8.3 Windows host testable.
#
# A fixture that must carry a carriage return in its committed bytes belongs
# under an explicit `-text` rule; none exists, and `just vv` compares the
# committed bytes, so one that appeared without the rule would fail the gate.
* text=auto eol=lf
